<section class="page-hero page-hero-simple growing-hero"><div><span class="eyebrow">GROW WHAT FITS YOUR PLACE</span><h1>A fruit garden<br><em>begins with the site.</em></h1><p>Match your fruit plant to climate, sunlight, drainage, pollination and the space it needs as an adult. A well-chosen plant is usually easier to care for than a difficult plant in the wrong place.</p><a href="/guide/growing-fruit-at-home" class="button-primary">Open the growing guide <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="simple-hero-side"><span class="big-serif-mark">G</span><span class="side-rule"></span><p>Site first<br>Selection second</p></div></section>
<section class="section-block"><div class="section-heading-row"><div><span class="eyebrow">THE PLANTING DECISION</span><h2>Six questions before planting</h2><p>Use these as a first filter, then check local horticultural advice for the specific fruit.</p></div></div><div class="grow-question-grid"><article><h3>What is your climate?</h3><p>Consider winter lows, summer heat, humidity, rainfall and winter chill requirements for temperate plants.</p></article><article><h3>How much sunlight?</h3><p>Many fruit crops perform best with strong light. Shade can reduce flowering, fruit set and flavour development.</p></article><article><h3>Does water drain?</h3><p>Roots need water, but many fruit trees suffer in persistently waterlogged soil. Match drainage and irrigation to the species.</p></article><article><h3>Is it self-fertile?</h3><p>Some plants need a compatible partner or pollinator activity. Check bloom timing and pollination requirements first.</p></article><article><h3>What is mature size?</h3><p>Rootstock, pruning and growth habit affect height and spread. Plan for access, light, roots and future harvest.</p></article><article><h3>How will you harvest?</h3><p>Consider thorniness, fruit drop, ripening concentration, storage and whether the harvest is reachable without risky climbing.</p></article></div></section>
<section class="grow-choices"><div class="grow-choice-copy"><span class="eyebrow eyebrow-light">CHOOSE THE RIGHT PROFILE</span><h2>Species is the beginning.<br>Cultivar is the decision.</h2><p>Within a species, named cultivars can differ in maturity date, flavour, size, chill need, disease resistance and pollination. Confirm the details for your locality before buying a plant.</p><a href="/guide/understanding-cultivar-names" class="light-link">Understand cultivar names <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div><div class="grow-choice-side"><h3>Keep a garden record</h3><p>Write down the plant name, cultivar, rootstock if known, source, planting date, flowering time and first harvest. This makes future pruning and comparisons more reliable.</p><a href="/glossary/rootstock" class="text-link">Learn the term “rootstock” <span aria-hidden="true">↗</span></a></div></section>
